    "narrative": "The highlight of today\u2019s card is undoubtedly **Corbin Carroll**, who leads our squad with an elite efficiency score of 82.99. Even against a capable arm like Michael King, **Corbin Carroll**\u2019s projected output is buoyed by his leadoff spot and a high stolen base probability, which remains our primary focus for closing the gap in 5x5 categories. Right behind him, **Brice Turang** earns a 73.39 score; while he faces a tough defensive test against an elite throwing catcher, the combination of his leadoff role and the opposing pitcher\u2019s slow delivery makes him a high-upside play for a multi-steal performance. We are also leaning on **Isaac Paredes** (72.13) against Bieber, as the favorable xERA matchup and his prime #3 spot in the order provide a significant boost to his floor, effectively masking any park-induced regression.\n\nOur outfield configuration leans heavily into favorable matchups, with **James Wood** (66.48) and **Seiya Suzuki** (66.01) both offering strong value. **James Wood** continues to flash elite xwOBA metrics, and despite facing Nola, he is essentially an auto-start at the top of the lineup. **Mickey Moniak** remains our most marginal start at 49.81; he earns a significant park factor boost, but the platoon disadvantage against Seymour keeps his ceiling in check. **Jose Caballero** (54.15) was the final man into the lineup; while he lacks the elite pop of our top-tier options, his capacity to swipe bags on any given night against McGreevy keeps him relevant. \n\nThe bench today is largely a reflection of a light schedule, as **Josh Naylor**, **Tyler Soderstrom**, and **Austin Riley** are all inactive. The most critical development is the continued absence of **Vinnie Pasquantino** due to his IL designation, a major drain on our depth. We have been forced to cycle through prospects like **Henry Bolte**, **Dalton Rushing**, and **Travis Bazzana** to monitor for potential high-upside call-ups, though for today, the math was clear: play the healthy, high-sprint-speed options and protect our games-played caps where a platoon disadvantage threatened to drag a player's efficiency below the 40-point threshold. We will continue to monitor the status of **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Gabriel Moreno**, **Max Muncy**, **Zach McKinstry**, **Cole Young**, and **Edwin Arroyo** to see if any late-breaking lineup changes necessitate a pivot before first pitch.",
